## Design Notes

### Why not keep two paths?
Performance is a north star. scanner-rs was purposefully designed to be the fastest
secret scanner on the market. The scanner-scheduler executor (work-stealing, io_uring,
CPU affinity, cache-aligned buffers) is where that performance lives. Having distributed
mode bypass it through ReadConnector::open() per-item reads would create a performance
cliff. One path, two entry points.

### Why ScanDriver instead of FileSource for everything?
`run_git_scan()` at `scratch-scanner-rs/src/git_scan/runner.rs:1007-1016` is a
completely separate entry point from `scan_local()`. Git scanning manages commit
walking, tree diffing, pack parsing, and blob dedup with its own lifecycle —
fundamentally different from the FS path. Forcing git through FileSource would
either lose performance or require a fake adapter.

### What happens to existing gossip-scan-pipeline tests?
The scan loop has extensive invariant tests (SL1-SL8: validate-before-persist,
process-before-persist, etc.). These invariants are re-expressed against the new
ShardRunner coordination wrapper (see §5 above).

### Future source types (S3, GitHub, Confluence, etc.)
New sources implement ScanSourceFactory + ScanDriver. Each driver owns its I/O
within `ScanDriver::run()`. The executor's work-stealing and threading is shared
across all source types. Future drivers must satisfy Stage 3 conformance
equivalents (see §6 above).

### Why CommitSink is separate from EventOutput (Decision 19)
EventOutput handles raw scan events (findings, metrics, progress). CommitSink
handles the per-item commit lifecycle with durability guarantees. Conflating
the two would either burden CLI mode with commit protocol overhead or weaken
distributed mode's ordering guarantees. Separate concerns, separate traits.

### Why finish_item() must be non-blocking (Decision 23)
Scan threads are the throughput bottleneck. If `finish_item()` blocks on I/O
(e.g., writing to the done ledger), scan threads stall and throughput collapses.
The sink must enqueue the finish request and return immediately. A dedicated
committer pool handles durability asynchronously. This is the same pattern used
by high-throughput message brokers (Kafka produce callbacks, io_uring SQE
submission).

